ation and sort of like an opiate  or a sense of well it's not so bad I'm  gonna hang out with you philosophy can  actually get to the root of things and  make him feel not only better not only  okay but actually happy there's a  another key theme about the goods of  fortune are they really the goods that  we take them to be wealth property fame  power are these things really as good as  we make them out to be and then he's  going to talk about the nature of  genuine happiness what is it that will  really satisfy it satisfy us what will  really make us happy it's going to turn  out that that is actually God and again  this is not a a Christian tract although  it's very compatible with Christianity  this is coming from ancient philosophy  he's drawing on Plato he's drawing on  Aristotle he's drawing on the Stoics  another key idea is going to be that  that God providentially orders  everything and then it raises another  connected question  well then wines are things so screwed up  what is what is going on with evil why  do evil people seem to prosper what is  the nature of evil finally we're going  to talk about one other issue the  compatibility between two things that  seem to be totally irreconcilable that  is divine foreknowledge God being will  see what's going to happen in the future  and human free will  are these really compatible or aren't  they so those are key themes that we're  gonna see being worked out in this book  again you know one last thing to say if  you have to work your way through this  and you're a little bit short on time I  would just skip over the poetry because  there's so much meat there in the  philosophical dialogue so much rich fare  that it's going to take you a little bit  of time to to digest that that will be  enough to occupy
 

